<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main points in a simple audio recording aimed at a general audience. Interactive: Organize information from group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow the main points in a simple audio recording about study tips and academic advice, identifying key strategies and examples (e.g., "Lucia said that if I studied regularly, I would get better grades." "You should take breaks while studying to stay focused."). Can identify specific details in a motivational talk on skill development, focusing on future advice (e.g., "The speaker said that if you practice public speaking, you will gain more confidence." "You should set clear goals for the trimester."). Can organize information from a group discussion on academic goals, summarizing advice given by peers (e.g., "Kathy suggested that you could manage your time better, and Mariana added that if you joined a study group, you might improve your understanding.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing core vocabulary and pronunciation for the lesson's listening focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a picture of a 9th-grade student looking stressed while studying and asks, "What challenges might this student be facing?" and "What advice would you give them to improve their academic achievement?" Students brainstorm ideas in small groups for 2 minutes and share briefly, activating prior knowledge on the theme. (Sociolinguistic: Facilitating peer group discussions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words related to academic achievement and advice: <i>achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, strategy, discipline, success, motivation</i>. The teacher says each word clearly, models its pronunciation, and writes it on the board. Special attention is given to pronunciation of contrastive sounds like /gr/ in "growth" and /k/ in "skill," and stress patterns for words like "PROgress" (noun) vs. "proGRESS" (verb). For instance, the teacher demonstrates "proGRESS" (verb) vs. "PROgress" (noun) with hand gestures. (Pronunciation: Practice contrastive sounds and stress patterns).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'motivation' something that makes you want to do something, or a study technique?" (Expected answer: "Something that makes you want to do something.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you show 'discipline' in your study, does it mean you study whenever you want or you follow a strict plan?" (Expected answer: "You follow a strict plan.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development" through an audio text. Focused listening for main ideas and specific details.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (approx. 2 minutes) of a senior student or a counselor giving advice to a ninth grader on achieving academic success. The recording incorporates the target vocabulary (e.g., <i>goals, strategy, practice, improvement, focus</i>) and grammatical structures (modals for advice, second conditional). Genre: Motivational talk / Academic advice; Register: semi-formal, encouraging; Format: audio podcast segment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and interpreting oral messages, identifying key strategies and examples from spoken advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listen (Gist):</b> Students listen once and answer a general question: "What is the main purpose of this audio? Is it to tell a story, give instructions, or offer advice?" (Expected answer: "Offer advice.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listen (Specific Details):</b> Students listen again and complete True/False statements based on the audio content. Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The speaker suggests that if you procrastinate, you will feel less stress." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should set clear goals for each trimester to track your progress." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you join a study group, you might improve your understanding." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Controlled and semi-controlled activities to reinforce target grammar and vocabulary through listening and rephrasing tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reinforcing second conditional for giving advice (e.g., "If I were more organized, I would achieve my academic goals."), modals for giving advice (e.g., "You should complete your assignments on time."), and future simple for first conditional (e.g., "If you focus on your studies, you will achieve your academic goals."). Using target vocabulary: <i>improve, study, practice, organize, focus, advise, achieve, develop, recommend, suggest, plan, track, discuss</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a transcript of parts of the audio with blanks. They listen again carefully and fill in the missing words (target vocabulary or parts of the grammar structures). E.g., "The speaker said that if I ______ (study) regularly, I ______ (would / get) better grades." "You ______ (should / create) a study ______ (schedule) to stay on track." The teacher pauses the audio as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Rephrasing Advice – Listening):</b> The teacher reads short sentences describing common academic problems. Students listen and, in pairs, rephrase advice using "You should..." or "If I were you, I would..." and appropriate vocabulary. E.g., Teacher: "A student finds it hard to focus during online classes." (Students discuss and respond: "You should try to find a quiet place to study." "If I were that student, I would set short-term goals for each session to improve my focus.") (Pragmatic: Giving study tips).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check (Listening for Accuracy):</b> Students exchange their completed gap-fill exercises from activity 1. The teacher plays the audio one last time, and students listen and check their partner's answers for accuracy in vocabulary and grammar, specifically noting correct use of 'would,' 'should,' and present/past verb forms in the conditionals. They identify any pronunciation challenges they notice in their peer's reading of the sentences. (Sociolinguistic: Providing clear, actionable advice to support peers).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development," focusing on interactive listening and organizing information from peers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group discussion (3-4 students) followed by a collaborative summary. Students will play the role of senior students giving advice to a hypothetical ninth grader struggling with various academic challenges (e.g., time management, lack of motivation, difficulty understanding concepts).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students engage in "My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is..." by discussing and synthesizing the best advice for "Academic Achievement and Skill Development."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Scenario Introduction:</b> The teacher presents 3-4 challenge cards (e.g., "struggling with procrastination," "low grades in English," "difficulty setting goals"). Each group chooses one or two.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Group Discussion (Listening for Advice):</b> Students discuss and offer advice for the chosen challenges, using modals (<i>should, must, can</i>) and the second conditional (<i>If I were you, I would...; If you practiced more, you would...</i>). One student in each group acts as a 'listener-recorder,' taking brief notes on the main advice given by their peers. They listen specifically for key vocabulary like <i>strategy, focus, discipline, improvement, time management</i>. (Sociolinguistic: Facilitating peer group discussions; Pragmatic: Giving study tips).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Summary:</b> Based on the notes taken, each group works together to create a short "Top 3 Advice" list for their chosen challenge, summarizing the advice they *heard* from each other. They must use phrases like "Kathy suggested that you could manage your time better," and "Mariana added that if you joined a study group, you might improve your understanding." (Listening: Organize information from group discussions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in expressing and understanding advice, collaborative interaction, and accurate comprehension of peer contributions. The primary listening skill here is active listening to synthesize and organize information from multiple speakers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to evaluate listening comprehension, organization of information, and appropriate language use. Student self-assessment reflecting on learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and summary creation. Peer assessment during summary presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group orally presents their "Top 3 Advice" list to the class. While one group presents, other students listen attentively and note down one new piece of advice they found particularly useful. The teacher observes how well students organized the information they listened to from their peers and their use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation & Peer Check):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of academic and advice-related vocabulary (e.g., correct stress in 'PROgress' vs. 'proGRESS').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Appropriate inclusion of at least 5-7 new words (e.g., <i>strategy, focus, discipline, motivation</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Clear organization and summarization of listened-to advice from peers, using appropriate linking phrases.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct application of modals for advice (<i>should, must, can</i>) and second conditional (<i>If... I would...</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Evidence of active listening and contribution to the group's summary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two questions on an exit slip:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new piece of advice you heard today (from the audio or your classmates) that you think is most useful for a ninth grader?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did you understand the main points of the audio recording about study tips? (Circle one: Very Well / Mostly / A Little / Not at all)"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson, self-assessment by students and teacher, and connection to the next lesson in the theme sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> Students consider the following questions:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary words did you learn today, and how will they help you discuss academic advice?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to the advice and organizing it today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the listening activities worked well for students to follow main points and identify details? Was the audio level and speed appropriate for B1.1?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully use the target vocabulary and grammar (modals, second conditional) when rephrasing and summarizing advice? What adjustments are needed for future vocabulary or grammar reinforcement?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I scaffold the next lesson (Reading Comprehension) to build upon the vocabulary and concepts introduced in this listening lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "Great job discussing and listening to advice today! In our next lesson, we will read an article about successful study habits for academic achievement, using many of the same vocabulary words we learned and practiced today. Pay attention to how the authors use these words in a written context."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>